WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:06.151
The shock wave hit it,

00:00:06.151 --> 00:00:09.034
caused the refrigerator to get lofted,

00:00:09.034 --> 00:00:11.610
and now it's moving faster
than the shock wave.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:12.900
Well, that just can't happen.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.850
My name is Greg Spriggs.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.670
I work at the Lawrence
Livermore National Laboratory.

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:18.840
Prior to that, I worked at the

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.500
Los Alamos National Laboratory
in the critical mass lab.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.760
Today we're going to be
looking at nuclear explosions

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.890
and trying to judge
how realistic they are.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:28.920
What are you doing?

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.921
Batman: I can get it out over the bay.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:38.940
Actually, that was one of the better ones.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.697
It had the right shape,
and it had the glow time.

00:00:41.697 --> 00:00:43.705
The fireball eventually cools,

00:00:43.705 --> 00:00:46.470
and it cools very rapidly
by radiative heat transfer.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:49.680
So, the length of time that
the fireball continues to glow

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.930
is referred to as the glow time.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.521
It's out over the bay.
It's clear of the city.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:59.985
These mushroom clouds can go up as high as

00:00:59.985 --> 00:01:01.335
40,000 feet in the air,

00:01:01.335 --> 00:01:03.570
so you'd be able to see it from way off.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:05.460
There'd be no danger for nuclear fallout

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:08.160
unless the wind was blowing
directly towards them.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.480
So, there's a general rule of thumb.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.650
If you look at the cloud, the
mushroom cap, if it's white,

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.530
then you will have zero
fallout, for the most part.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:17.746
If it looks dirty,

00:01:17.746 --> 00:01:19.754
then it means that dirt
got into the fireball,

00:01:19.754 --> 00:01:21.000
and now there's a carry material,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.910
which will bring the
nuclear bomb debris down

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:25.260
and create local fallout.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:28.653
So if you detonated over the
water, very little fallout.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:31.710
The people that were
standing on the bridge

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.450
kind of felt a breeze.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.310
That happened a lot too soon.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:36.870
It would've taken a finite length of time

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.610
for that shock wave to make it out there.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:39.990
And I would give it a seven.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:41.081
The flash was good,

00:01:41.081 --> 00:01:43.164
and the shape of the detonation was good.

00:01:43.164 --> 00:01:45.722
[beeping]

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.810
It went off, and then this
plasma was squirting out

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:52.920
through that fissure.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.660
Potentially, if they
actually could do that,

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:55.860
it would break up the asteroid

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.900
if the asteroid is small enough.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.240
It would be very difficult
if you had a large asteroid

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.603
to get a weapon big enough in yield

00:02:02.603 --> 00:02:04.380
to cause it to completely break up.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:07.238
That would be a very
difficult thing to do.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:17.040
In outer space, of course,
there is no shock wave,

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.800
and if there was, why
would it be disk-shaped?

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.300
It's a spherical shock wave.

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:22.625
There would be a bright flash,

00:02:22.625 --> 00:02:25.530
and everything would cool
within 20 microseconds,

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.360
so you wouldn't be seeing this

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.598
long glow time that they show in the movie

00:02:29.598 --> 00:02:32.700
and people on Earth looking up
and seeing this white flash.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:33.816
You would see the flash,

00:02:33.816 --> 00:02:36.616
but it would only last
for 10 to 20 microseconds.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.580
If it's small enough and
you could break it up,

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:47.100
then it could possibly
dissipate in the atmosphere.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.180
It could burn itself out.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:52.350
These two halves, yeah, that
would be very unpredictable.

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:53.820
For realism, I'd give it a one.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:55.770
If you went in and actually
drilled into an asteroid

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:57.333
and tried to break it up, how do you know

00:02:57.333 --> 00:02:58.625
what it's going to break up into

00:02:58.625 --> 00:03:00.930
and where are the pieces going to fly?

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.297
This is a matter of life and death.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.420
They were trying to hold
all the cables in place.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.920
This was a test. They weren't certain

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:11.695
that this particular
design was going to work,

00:03:11.695 --> 00:03:13.410
and so this was sort of the prototype,

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:15.020
and they didn't go to great lengths

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:18.757
to make everything really robust.

00:03:18.757 --> 00:03:21.930
So they were just basically
winging it with duct tape

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:23.790
to hold things in place.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:27.450
And the actual one probably was
a little bit more structured

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.330
and more engineered to be a real weapon.

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:33.840
The reason they suspended
the bomb from the tower,

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:35.465
they wanted to get it above the ground

00:03:35.465 --> 00:03:37.230
so they could measure the shock wave.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:40.500
There was a lot of uncertainty,
since it was the first one.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.870
There was a lot of uncertainty
as to whether this would work

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:46.013
and if it did work, how much
energy would be released.

00:03:46.013 --> 00:03:48.210
And so they needed to
be able to film this,

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.040
and they didn't want the shock wave

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:51.900
interacting with the surface.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.030
They thought that if they
suspended it up high enough,

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:56.207
that they would kind of suppress

00:03:56.207 --> 00:03:59.070
the amount of nuclear
fallout that would occur

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:00.633
by all the dirt being lofted.

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:03.657
Detonator's charged.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:08.850
They had actually built several shelters

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:10.440
for the scientists.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:13.320
I think the closest shelter
was about 5 miles away.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:15.240
And of course everybody
was wearing goggles.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.880
When the detonation goes off,
it would look very bright,

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:19.260
but it would protect their eyes.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:22.440
Here are the glasses. Very, very dark.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:25.170
Right now, I can't see anything.
It's pitch black in here.

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:27.988
But if a nuclear detonation
went off, I could see it.

00:04:27.988 --> 00:04:32.987
Three, two, one.

00:04:32.987 --> 00:04:35.250
They were all laying down
thinking that the shock wave

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.050
might get to them and
if they were laying down

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:38.760
that it wouldn't be a direct hit.

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:40.830
You don't want to have a big surface area

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:42.060
if the shock wave is coming over.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:43.440
You want to be kind of laying flat.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:47.040
There have been situations
where we've had tests

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:48.540
where the yield was a little bit higher

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:51.300
than what people thought
or the wind blew the bomb

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.520
a little bit closer to the observers,

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.190
that people have actually gotten
a little bit of a sunburn.

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:58.793
I don't think the sunscreen
would've helped very much.

00:04:58.793 --> 00:05:01.620
It's basically a heat flux that hits you.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:04.770
I guess it would've helped
a little bit, but not much.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:07.200
For the accuracy of what
the weapon looked like

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.660
and how they hung it from
the tower and so forth,

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:11.612
maybe a seven.

00:05:11.612 --> 00:05:13.662
Oh, that can't be good at all.

00:05:16.806 --> 00:05:19.418
Back in the '50s, they
were very, very conscious

00:05:19.418 --> 00:05:22.530
of showing the nuclear weapons.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:25.500
The weapon that was
hanging off of that tower,

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:28.200
they actually had what
were called tower cabs

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:29.970
that were built up at the top.

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:32.790
The cab itself had either plywood walls

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:34.230
or had some sort of curtain.

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:35.638
They were paranoid that

00:05:35.638 --> 00:05:37.838
some other nation was going to fly over

00:05:37.838 --> 00:05:39.900
and take a photograph of
what the bomb looked like.

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:42.840
The bomb was just sitting in
there hanging off of a cable.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:45.866
Again, not quite true to history.

00:05:46.725 --> 00:05:49.849
Loudspeaker: Seven, six,

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:52.499
four, three.

00:05:52.499 --> 00:05:56.160
The lead-lined refrigerator
kind of struck me odd.

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:57.810
Everybody knows about lead poisoning.

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:00.330
Why would you have a
refrigerator with lead in it?

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:04.530
You would need several
feet thickness of lead

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.180
in order to actually
knock down the radiation

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:08.550
to the point where he might've
been able to survive it.

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:10.458
If the house had a basement,

00:06:10.458 --> 00:06:12.450
I would've gone out,
gotten into the basement.

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:14.310
That's probably the only
thing that would've saved him

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:16.535
from the thermal blast
and from the shock wave.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.930
Got several of the physics things correct.

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:26.013
The first thing that happens

00:06:26.013 --> 00:06:28.913
after a nuclear detonation occurs

00:06:28.913 --> 00:06:33.480
is that you get a large
burst of prompt radiation.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:35.173
You get neutrons and prompt gammas

00:06:35.173 --> 00:06:37.200
coming off of the fissioning process.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:39.122
You don't necessarily see that.

00:06:39.122 --> 00:06:41.069
An observer might actually
feel it if they were

00:06:41.069 --> 00:06:44.310
in close enough, and that
would be a bad thing.

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:48.720
The next thing is that there's
a very intense thermal pulse.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:51.810
You get this very intense thermal blast

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:53.112
from a nuclear detonation

00:06:53.112 --> 00:06:56.700
that you do not get with
a conventional explosive.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:59.730
And so anything that's close
to that detonation point,

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:02.640
such as the mannequins
that they had standing out

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:05.970
in the front yards, those
would be incinerated.

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:07.500
The timescale was a little wrong.

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:09.960
That incineration occurs
very, very quickly.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:12.570
Those mannequins would've
disappeared into a puff of smoke

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:15.087
within a couple tenths of a second.

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:18.860
As for the glass,

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:20.595
the thermal pulse would've
gone right through it,

00:07:20.595 --> 00:07:23.040
so the glass probably
wouldn't have shattered.

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:24.840
And it's not until the
shock wave gets there

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:26.999
that things actually get smashed.

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:38.489
Here you have a shock wave,

00:07:38.489 --> 00:07:41.640
and the car is keeping in front of it,

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:43.305
and then the refrigerator's
actually moving

00:07:43.305 --> 00:07:44.730
faster than the shock wave.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:45.830
The shock wave hit it,

00:07:45.830 --> 00:07:49.371
caused the refrigerator to get lofted,

00:07:49.371 --> 00:07:51.840
and now it's moving faster
than the shock wave.

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:53.310
Well, that just can't happen.

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:55.980
As the shock wave goes out,
it eventually degenerates

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:58.260
into what we call an acoustic wave.

00:07:58.260 --> 00:07:59.880
It's traveling at the speed of sound.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:02.160
So imagine this refrigerator
hitting the ground

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:04.740
going at 760 miles an hour.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:06.810
I don't think it has
the structural strength,

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:07.877
even with the lead in it,

00:08:07.877 --> 00:08:10.290
to withstand that kind of an impact.

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:12.630
When the shock first goes
out, it's moving outward,

00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:16.740
and it leaves behind a
very low-density bubble

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:17.820
where the detonation occurred,

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:20.130
which causes a huge buoyant force.

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:22.350
And as that bubble starts to rise,

00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:23.670
it sucks everything back in.

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:25.320
So this is called the negative phase.

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:28.080
So, in this case, Indy would
not have been thrown out,

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:29.160
but he would've been thrown out

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:31.140
and then sucked back in again.

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:34.320
I would give it a three
in terms of realism.

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:36.120
He got the thermal pulse right,

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:40.440
and he got the positive phase
of the shock wave correct.

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:43.488
But the rest of it kind of fell apart.

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:52.292
The subsidence crater is produced

00:08:52.292 --> 00:08:54.630
by a cavity that's created underground.

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:57.450
When the detonation goes off,
it vaporizes the material,

00:08:57.450 --> 00:09:01.380
but the material's still there,
it's just in a plasma state.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:05.970
And there's a lot of air
spaces in dirt and in rock.

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:07.860
And what happens basically is you get this

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:11.730
very high-temperature, very
high-pressure region underneath,

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:14.970
in the cavity, and it
compresses everything around it.

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:17.790
It's kind of like taking
a thick piece of Styrofoam

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:21.990
and stepping on it. And it
squishes this dirt and rock,

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:25.170
surrounding dirt, into
this higher, denser layer,

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:27.081
which leaves the cavity behind.

00:09:27.081 --> 00:09:28.866
[beeping]

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:36.340
This is one of the problems

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:39.162
with these nuclear
detonations in the movies,

00:09:39.162 --> 00:09:42.870
is they try to incorporate
everything into one shot.

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:44.970
Where did that bright
flash of light come from?

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:47.586
If it was underground, you
wouldn't see a bright flash.

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:57.210
There certainly is a shock
wave in the Earth's crust,

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:59.100
and it moves very, very fast.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:02.400
The way it was portrayed
in this film clip,

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:05.310
it looks like it's some
sort of a tidal wave of dirt

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:08.370
bulging up and kind of
moving along at a slow pace.

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:09.840
And they've actually filmed this.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:13.500
They did approximately 800
tests in Nevada Test Site.

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:16.020
After they stopped doing
atmospheric testing,

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:18.060
they decided they would do
all the testing underground.

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:20.520
There's no tidal wave of the dirt

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:21.840
bunching up the way they showed it.

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.660
And there would be no shock
wave coming out of the ground

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:27.260
that would damage a
helicopter flying over.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:34.290
Shock wave took down the damn chopper.

00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:36.810
That's EMP, electromagnetic pulse.

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:39.060
You don't get an EMP unless
you have a detonation

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:40.260
high in the atmosphere,

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:43.050
which then can interfere
with communication,

00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:44.580
electronic equipment on the earth.

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:47.310
You generate these high
currents in the atmosphere.

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:49.680
You would not get that with
a detonation underground.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:53.310
They were trying to take all
of the basically understood

00:10:53.310 --> 00:10:54.966
nuclear-weapon effects and put it into

00:10:54.966 --> 00:10:57.641
one detonation underground.

00:10:57.641 --> 00:10:59.024
I would also give it a one.

00:10:59.024 --> 00:11:00.543
It certainly wasn't realistic.

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:10.290
If you took a nuclear
weapon, you detonated it

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:12.870
out in true outer space,
not in the upper atmosphere,

00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:15.687
but in outer space, what happens is

00:11:15.687 --> 00:11:18.335
the bomb would vaporize
because of all the energy

00:11:18.335 --> 00:11:21.248
that would be deposited
in the weapon itself.

00:11:21.248 --> 00:11:23.430
There is no shock wave in outer space.

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:26.580
Shock wave can only occur
when you have this disturbance

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:28.638
moving through some other medium.

00:11:28.638 --> 00:11:32.063
And they kind of show this ball of gas,

00:11:32.063 --> 00:11:34.230
which, that would not be associated

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:36.333
with the nuclear detonation itself.

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:38.610
Too much mass involved with it.

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:40.740
And also, it would cool
almost immediately.

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:43.950
When you're in outer space, you
get these radiative cooling,

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:45.720
and you would see a bright flash,

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:50.220
and it would last for maybe
10 to 20 microseconds,

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:52.950
and then it would go completely black.

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:54.960
Now, the fact that they put this inside of

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:57.150
some sort of a structure
might have been their fuel

00:11:57.150 --> 00:11:59.460
that was actually exploding there

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:00.870
and not the nuclear detonation.

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:04.530
You know, if this
explosion was propagating

00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:06.305
down the length of this spaceship,

00:12:06.305 --> 00:12:08.070
then OK, it lasts longer.

00:12:08.070 --> 00:12:10.260
But if it were an
instantaneous kind of thing,

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:11.700
it would cool almost immediately.

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:12.840
It was my least favorite.

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:14.275
I'd give it a one.

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:22.110
I have never even seen that before.

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:24.930
I don't even know what would
cause a hole like that.

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:26.580
I'm not sure where they came up with that.

00:12:33.990 --> 00:12:37.560
If they had detonated
this much deeper down,

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:39.060
you may not have gotten a cloud at all.

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:40.620
It's sort of like an underground shot.

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:41.692
If you go deep enough,

00:12:41.692 --> 00:12:44.280
you really don't see the
effect on the surface at all.

00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:45.750
When the detonation goes off,

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:47.910
it creates this low-density region,

00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:50.670
and it basically would be a big
bubble underneath the water.

00:12:50.670 --> 00:12:52.650
And that has a buoyant effect.

00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:55.495
And as it pops up, it
causes this waterspout.

00:12:56.983 --> 00:12:59.525
They do have the shock wave moving out,

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:02.970
and the water spout does kind of form.

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:05.370
These kind of shots do produce waves.

00:13:05.370 --> 00:13:07.890
They're not very big waves,
but they do produce waves.

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:11.793
That's not something that would
destroy an aircraft carrier.

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:18.523
The shock wave would've
traveled through the water,

00:13:18.523 --> 00:13:20.280
and then it would hit
the hull of the ship.

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:21.930
And there's a possibility
that when you hit it

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:24.390
on the side like that with
a really strong shock wave,

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:27.780
that it could cause antennas
on the deck to break.

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:29.370
But certainly there
wouldn't be a shock wave

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:33.540
above the surface that was
causing that to tip over.

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:34.890
During Operation Crossroads,

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:38.070
which was the first
detonation after World War II,

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:41.310
they wanted to see what
the effect would be

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:45.450
on the Navy fleet if a detonation
were to occur underwater.

00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:49.170
So they took a lot of the
destroyers and aircraft carriers,

00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:52.110
and they put them in the
middle of the Bikini Atoll,

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:53.700
and they detonated the shot under it.

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:55.920
The other ships ended up
getting a lot of damage

00:13:55.920 --> 00:13:57.540
from the nuclear fallout.

00:13:57.540 --> 00:13:59.187
Again, it was close enough to the surface

00:13:59.187 --> 00:14:02.790
that probably the shock
wave would've hit the ships,

00:14:02.790 --> 00:14:05.010
hit the hull, and could
have caused damage.

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:07.830
But for the most part, it was
mostly radioactive material

00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:09.871
that settled onto the decks.

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:16.380
Some shock wave emerging
from underneath the water

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:18.540
and then propagating through the air

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:22.320
and knocking the helicopter
out, probably not realistic.

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:23.915
But again, I mean, if it were close enough

00:14:23.915 --> 00:14:26.850
and it became part of
that waterspout, yeah,

00:14:26.850 --> 00:14:28.710
it would certainly take
out the helicopter.

00:14:28.710 --> 00:14:31.620
Again, I would give it a
low mark here. Maybe a two.

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:34.261
No, sir. It is not a
thing a sane man would do.

00:14:34.261 --> 00:14:35.520
The doomsday machine is designed

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:37.290
to trigger itself automatically.

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:40.770
I have no knowledge of any
doomsday device in existence.

00:14:40.770 --> 00:14:42.545
This is a fabrication of Hollywood.

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:45.471
Woohoo!

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:50.940
They actually have to put
the weapon on a parachute

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:52.680
to give the plane time
to get out of the way.

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:54.930
So as soon as they dropped the bomb,

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:57.690
the bomb would drop,
free-fall for a while,

00:14:57.690 --> 00:15:00.210
the parachute would open
and start going down slowly.

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:02.790
In the meantime, the plane
would do a hard right,

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:04.500
go at 90 degrees, and that would make

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:08.370
the distance between the
detonation point and the plane

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:10.590
the largest distance when
it actually went off.

00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:12.720
It has to be far enough away
from the detonation point

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:15.616
that the shock wave
doesn't destroy the plane.

00:15:15.616 --> 00:15:16.707
Woohoo!

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:27.450
Film clip of the detonation was real.

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:31.050
So he did go to the trouble
of putting that into the film.

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:32.670
I would give the detonation a 10,

00:15:32.670 --> 00:15:36.561
and I would give the
rest of it sort of a two.

